%N A401509 Numbers whose largest exponentially odd unitary divisor is a cube.
%C A401509 Differs from A353485 by having the terms 27, 125, 343, ..., and not having the terms 32, 128, 288, ... .
%C A401509 Number k such that A079978(A350389(k)) = 1.
%C A401509 Numbers whose prime factorization exponents are all congruent to {0, 2, 3, 4} mod 6 (A047229).
%C A401509 Numbers whose odd prime factorization exponents are all multiples of 3.
%H A401509 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A401509/b401509.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401509 <a href="/index/Pow#powerful">Index entries for sequences related to powerful numbers</a>.
%F A401509 Sum_{n>=1} 1/a(n) = zeta(6) * Product_{p prime} (1 + 1/p^2 + 1/p^3 + 1/p^4) = 1.88402388210108796766... .
%t A401509 q[n_] := n == 1 || AllTrue[FactorInteger[n][[;; , 2]], EvenQ[#] || Divisible[#, 3] &]; Select[Range[1350], q]
%o A401509 (PARI) isok(k) = #select(e -> (e % 2) && (e % 3), factor(k)[, 2]) == 0;
